<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Langkat Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1568 CE – 1946 CE (approx.) · capital Tanjung Pura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Malay sultanate of North Sumatra that grew rich from tobacco and oil under Dutch rule, and ended in the social revolution of 1946.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Langkat was one of the Malay states of the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/deli-sultanate">Deli Sultanate</a>. Local tradition traces its royal line to the 16th century, and the register dates it from 1568. For long it was subject to Aceh and Siak. Its seat was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanjung-pura">Tanjung Pura</a> on the Batang Serangan River.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> recognised its rulers, who leased land for tobacco plantations. Oil was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telaga-said">Telaga Said</a> in 1885, the field that launched the Royal Dutch company. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Musa and his son Abdul Aziz grew wealthy and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azizi-mosque">Azizi Great Mosque</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanjung-pura">Tanjung Pura</a>. The court was also a centre of Malay letters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In March 1946, during the social revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-sumatra">East Sumatra</a>, armed groups attacked the Malay aristocracy. Members of the royal family were killed, among them the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-hamzah">Tengku Amir Hamzah</a>, and the sultanate ended. Langkat is now a regency of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/langkat-sultanate.jpg" alt="Langkat Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sultanate_of_Langkat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1568 CE – 1946 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tanjung Pura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Musa, Abdul Aziz Abdul Jalil Rahmat Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Langkat</dd></dl></aside></div>
